### What problem does Builder solve?

The telescoping-constructor problem: an object with many fields forces either a huge constructor whose arguments are easy to misorder, or many overloaded constructors.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// BAD: positional arguments, easy to misorder
// new Order("ORD-1", "ACC-001", "RELIANCE", 100, price, fee, "BUY", notes)
// Was 100 the quantity or the account number?
```

### How do you design a Builder for a trade order?

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public final class Order {
    private final String orderId;
    private final String accountId;
    private final String symbol;
    private final int quantity;
    private final BigDecimal price;
    private final BigDecimal fee;
    private final String orderType;
    private final String notes;

    private Order(Builder b) {
        this.orderId = b.orderId;
        this.accountId = b.accountId;
        this.symbol = b.symbol;
        this.quantity = b.quantity;
        this.price = b.price;
        this.fee = b.fee;
        this.orderType = b.orderType;
        this.notes = b.notes;
    }

    public static Builder builder() { return new Builder(); }

    public static class Builder {
        private String orderId;
        private String accountId;
        private String symbol;
        private int quantity;
        private BigDecimal price;
        private BigDecimal fee = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        private String orderType = "BUY";
        private String notes = "";

        public Builder orderId(String v) { this.orderId = v; return this; }
        public Builder accountId(String v) { this.accountId = v; return this; }
        public Builder symbol(String v) { this.symbol = v; return this; }
        public Builder quantity(int v) { this.quantity = v; return this; }
        public Builder price(BigDecimal v) { this.price = v; return this; }
        public Builder fee(BigDecimal v) { this.fee = v; return this; }
        public Builder orderType(String v) { this.orderType = v; return this; }
        public Builder notes(String v) { this.notes = v; return this; }

        public Order build() {
            if (orderId == null) throw new IllegalStateException("orderId required");
            if (accountId == null) throw new IllegalStateException("accountId required");
            if (symbol == null) throw new IllegalStateException("symbol required");
            if (quantity <= 0) throw new IllegalStateException("quantity must be positive");
            if (price == null || price.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalStateException("price must be positive");
            return new Order(this);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return orderId + ": " + orderType + " " + quantity + " " + symbol
            + " @ " + price + " fee=" + fee + " acc=" + accountId;
    }
}
```

### How do you use the Builder?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderBuilderDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Order order = Order.builder()
            .orderId("ORD-001")
            .accountId("ACC-RAJESH")
            .symbol("RELIANCE")
            .quantity(100)
            .price(new BigDecimal("2450.00"))
            .fee(new BigDecimal("50.00"))
            .orderType("BUY")
            .notes("Market order at open")
            .build();

        System.out.println(order);
    }
}
```

```text
ORD-001: BUY 100 RELIANCE @ 2450.00 fee=50.00 acc=ACC-RAJESH
```

### How do you design a Builder for a query?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public final class TransactionQuery {
    private final String accountId;
    private final LocalDate from;
    private final LocalDate to;
    private final List<String> types;
    private final int limit;

    private TransactionQuery(Builder b) {
        this.accountId = b.accountId;
        this.from = b.from;
        this.to = b.to;
        this.types = List.copyOf(b.types);
        this.limit = b.limit;
    }

    public static Builder builder(String accountId) {
        return new Builder(accountId);
    }

    public static class Builder {
        private final String accountId; // required
        private LocalDate from = LocalDate.now().minusDays(30);
        private LocalDate to = LocalDate.now();
        private List<String> types = new ArrayList<>();
        private int limit = 100;

        private Builder(String accountId) {
            this.accountId = accountId;
        }

        public Builder from(LocalDate v) { this.from = v; return this; }
        public Builder to(LocalDate v) { this.to = v; return this; }
        public Builder type(String v) { this.types.add(v); return this; }
        public Builder limit(int v) { this.limit = v; return this; }

        public TransactionQuery build() {
            if (from.isAfter(to)) throw new IllegalStateException("from must be before to");
            return new TransactionQuery(this);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Query[" + accountId + " " + from + ".." + to + " types=" + types + " limit=" + limit + "]";
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.time.LocalDate;

public class QueryBuilderDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransactionQuery query = TransactionQuery.builder("ACC-PRIYA")
            .from(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 1))
            .to(LocalDate.of(2024, 3, 31))
            .type("DEBIT")
            .type("TRANSFER")
            .limit(50)
            .build();

        System.out.println(query);
    }
}
```

```text
Query[ACC-PRIYA 2024-01-01..2024-03-31 types=[DEBIT, TRANSFER] limit=50]
```

### How does build() enforce validation?

```java
try {
    Order bad = Order.builder()
        .orderId("ORD-002")
        .quantity(-5) // invalid
        .build();
} catch (IllegalStateException e) {
    System.out.println("Rejected: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

```text
Rejected: symbol required
```

Invalid objects can never exist.

### How do you separate required from optional fields?

Required fields go in the builder's constructor; optional fields get fluent setters with defaults.

```java
// Required in builder constructor:
public static Builder builder(String accountId) {
    return new Builder(accountId); // cannot be forgotten
}

// Optional with defaults:
private int limit = 100;
public Builder limit(int v) { this.limit = v; return this; }
```

### Where does Builder appear in the real world?

- `StringBuilder` chains `append()` calls.
- `HttpRequest.newBuilder()` in Java 11+.
- Spring `UriComponentsBuilder`.
- Lombok `@Builder` annotation.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Builder shines for many optional fields and immutability with validation.
- For two required fields, a plain constructor is simpler.
- In banking, a validated, immutable order means the object is correct from birth and cannot be tampered with after creation.
